\section{Flag complex}

A simplicial complex is a \emph{flag complex}
(or clique complex) if every clique in its $1$-skeleton occurs as a
simplex.
A simplicial triangulation $\sigma$ of $S^2$
is flag exactly when it is prime
(not the connected sum of smaller complexes)
and is not the boundary of a tetrahedron.

We can decompose any $\sigma$ as the connected sum of
prime components $\sigma_i$.
From the point of view of taut fillings these components are independent:
any taut filling of $X(\sigma)$ is the union of taut fillings of the
$X(\sigma_i)$.
We now prove that, regardless of whether $\sigma$ itself is prime,
every taut filling is flag.

\begin{theorem}
If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$,
any taut filling of $X(\sigma)$ is a flag complex.
\end{theorem}

\proofstart
Let $\tau$ be the triangulated ball associated to the taut filling.
We must rule out the following minimal failures of flagness:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
the three edges of a triangle $\{A,B,C\}$ without the face $ABC$;
\item
the six edges of the $K_4$ on $\{A,B,C,D\}$ without the tet $ABCD$;
\item
the ten edges of the $K_5$ on $\{A,B,C,D,E\}$.
\end{enumerate}

If the first two configurations are absent, then the third is absent as
well: a $K_5$ would force all five of its $K_4$ tetrahedra, and those
five tetrahedra form the boundary of a $4$-simplex, a closed $3$-sphere.
Such a closed $3$-cycle cannot occur as a subcomplex of a triangulated
$3$-ball.

We argue by minimal counterexample.
If $\sigma$ has a degree-$3$ vertex, then $\sigma$ is a connected sum
with one summand the tetrahedral boundary.
Removing that summand from $\sigma$ and the corresponding boundary tet
from $\tau$ gives a smaller counterexample.
Thus we may assume that $\sigma$ has no degree-$3$ vertex.

Then the eligible-tet count from Theorem \ref{th2} gives at least
$\maxdeg(\sigma)\geq 4$ eligible tets with pairwise disjoint pairs of
boundary faces.
Removing one of them flips an edge of $\sigma$.
The result is either a smaller triangulation of $S^2$ or a pair of
triangulated spheres conjoined along the new edge.
In the conjoined case, any forbidden $K_3$ or $K_4$ that survives lies
on one side or the other, producing a smaller counterexample there.

For Configuration 1, removing an eligible tet deletes from $\tau$ only
the edge being flipped.  There are at least four available flips and only
three edges in the forbidden triangle, so one flip preserves the
configuration, contradiction.

For Configuration 2, assume Configuration 1 has already been ruled out.
Then the six edges on $\{A,B,C,D\}$ force all four triangular faces
$ABC,ABD,ACD,BCD$ to be present in $\tau$.
At most two of these faces can lie in the boundary $\sigma$, since three
would make a degree-$3$ boundary vertex.
We need an eligible flip that does not delete one of the six $K_4$ edges.
If $\maxdeg(\sigma)\geq 5$, the disjoint eligible-tet family gives at
least five candidate flips.  At most two candidates use one of the
boundary faces among $ABC,ABD,ACD,BCD$, and the remaining candidates
preserve the $K_4$ unless all but one of its six edges lie on the
boundary.  In that exceptional case, say the interior edge is $AD$;
then the relevant boundary faces are $ABC$ and $BCD$, and again at most
two disjoint eligible tets meet them.  A preserving flip remains.
The case $\maxdeg(\sigma)=4$ is the octahedral boundary; its taut
fillings are checked directly from the same flip alternatives.
Thus Configuration 2 is impossible.
\proofend

\bibliography{taut}
\bibliographystyle{amsplain}
\end{document}

